# =============================================================================
# .env.example - configuration template for the Excel Clone backend
# =============================================================================
# This file is the complete list of configuration keys the backend reads.
#
# Step 1 - copy this file to `.env` in the repository root, then edit the copy.
# See "Which values to change" below for which entries need a real value and
# which supplied defaults are already usable:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Step 2 - nothing further is required to load it. `backend/app/core/config.py`
# reads the repository-root `.env` into the process environment when it is
# imported, before any `Settings` object or Google Cloud client is constructed.
# See "How this file is loaded" below for the precedence rules, and "Other ways
# to deliver these keys" for the launcher, shell-export and container
# alternatives that also work.
#
# Keep `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` single-quoted below. Its value is a JSON array, and a
# POSIX shell that sources this file strips the inner double quotes; startup then
# fails with `SettingsError: error parsing env var "allowed_origins"`.
#
# The credential, project, host, bucket and path values below are placeholders
# and do not work as supplied. The remaining values are usable defaults. Review
# every value against the environment you are configuring.
#
# Do NOT commit `.env`. No tracked ignore rule in this repository covers the
# root `.env`, so a populated `.env` shows up as an untracked file and can be
# staged by accident. Check `git status` before every commit.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How this file is loaded
#
# `backend/app/core/config.py` loads the repository-root `.env` into the process
# environment when it is imported, before any `Settings` object or Google Cloud
# client is constructed. So every key below reaches the process, not only the
# ones that are `Settings` fields - that is what makes the Google Cloud client
# variables in SECTION C work from this file.
#
# The loader does NOT overwrite a variable that is already set in the
# environment. A real environment variable always wins over this file, so an
# exported value or a Kubernetes env entry silently takes precedence. If a value
# here appears to be ignored, check `printenv` first.
#
# The file is optional: with no `.env` present the application reads its
# configuration from the environment alone, which is how the deployed container
# is configured.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Other ways to deliver these keys
#
# Each of these also places the keys in the process environment, and works
# alongside or instead of the `.env` above:
#
#   1. Let the launcher read the file. `uvicorn --env-file` loads it into the
#      environment before it imports the application, using the python-dotenv
#      that ships with the `uvicorn[standard]` pin in
#      `backend/requirements.txt`. Run from the repository root:
#
#        bash or zsh:  PYTHONPATH=. uvicorn --env-file .env backend.app.main:app
#        PowerShell:   $env:PYTHONPATH='.'; uvicorn --env-file .env backend.app.main:app
#
#      `backend.app.main:app` is the module path that resolves; `PYTHONPATH` must
#      point at the repository root for the `backend.app.*` imports.
#
#   2. Export the keys yourself, then start the application. One export per key,
#      using the values from the sections below:
#
#        bash or zsh:  export PROJECT_ID=your-gcp-project-id
#                      export ALLOWED_ORIGINS='["http://localhost:3000"]'
#        PowerShell:   $env:PROJECT_ID='your-gcp-project-id'
#                      $env:ALLOWED_ORIGINS='["http://localhost:3000"]'
#
#      Wrap the JSON-array value in single quotes so its double quotes survive.
#      Do not load this file with `source` or `.`: a POSIX shell strips those
#      double quotes, and startup then fails with
#      `SettingsError: error parsing env var "allowed_origins"`.
#
#   3. Containers and Kubernetes. Pass the same keys as container environment
#      variables - `docker run --env-file .env`, or `env:` / `envFrom:` in the
#      pod spec. These consumers take each value verbatim, so write values
#      exactly as they appear below, add no shell quoting, and keep every
#      comment on its own line rather than after a value.
#
# Configuration is not the only prerequisite for a first successful run:
# `backend/app/main.py` imports `init_db` from `backend/app/db/database.py`,
# which does not define it, so the process raises `ImportError` at startup
# before any key below is read. That is a functional gap in the application
# module, not a configuration error.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which values to change
#
# Replace these nine project-, credential-, origin-, path- and resource-specific
# placeholders: `PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL`, `SECRET_KEY`,
# `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, `gcs_bucket_name`, `firebase_project_id`,
# `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT` and `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`. Each one is
# marked `Replace this value.` below.
#
# The other ten values are working defaults or deliberate settings and may be
# kept exactly as supplied or tuned for your environment: `ALGORITHM`,
# `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES`, `signed_url_expiry_minutes`, `db_sslmode`,
# `auth_token_verifier`, `auth_enforcement_enabled`, `rate_limit_enabled`,
# `rate_limit_default`, `rate_limit_write` and `csp_report_only`. The note above
# each of them states its default and the values it accepts.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How these keys are read
#
# Once loaded, the backend reads the SECTION B and SECTION C keys through
# `Settings` in `backend/app/core/config.py`, which uses the Pydantic v1
# `BaseSettings` API. Four consequences:
#
#   1. `Settings` reads the process environment, never this file directly. The
#      loader in `config.py` is what bridges the two, and it runs at import.
#
#   2. Key names match the `Settings` field names. Matching is case-insensitive,
#      so `gcs_bucket_name` and `GCS_BUCKET_NAME` both resolve to the same
#      field. The spellings below match the field declarations exactly.
#
#   3. List-typed keys are parsed as JSON. `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` must be a JSON
#      array. A bare comma-separated string fails at startup with
#      `SettingsError: error parsing env var "allowed_origins"`.
#
#   4. `get_settings()` builds a fresh `Settings` on every call. Keys consulted
#      inside a request path therefore take effect on the next request, while
#      keys consumed when middleware is constructed need a process or pod
#      restart.
#
# Keys marked REQUIRED have no default. Omitting any one of them raises a
# Pydantic `ValidationError` and the application does not start. Several keys are
# additionally validated for content, not just type - a permitted range, or an
# accepted-value grammar; each says so below, and a rejected value also raises
# `ValidationError` at startup.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# SECTION A - Core settings (REQUIRED, no defaults)
# =============================================================================

# REQUIRED. Google Cloud project ID. Used to build the Secret Manager resource
# path `projects/<PROJECT_ID>/secrets/<NAME>/versions/latest`. Not overwritten
# at runtime. Replace this value.
PROJECT_ID=your-gcp-project-id

# REQUIRED. SQLAlchemy connection URL for the Cloud SQL PostgreSQL 13 database.
# Overwritten on each `Settings` construction from Secret Manager secret
# `DATABASE_URL`.
# It must still be set here: Pydantic validates before the secret is fetched, so
# omitting it raises `ValidationError` even though the value is then replaced.
# Replace this value.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg2://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/main-database

# REQUIRED. Celery broker and result backend URL.
# Overwritten on each `Settings` construction from Secret Manager secret
# `REDIS_URL`.
# It must still be set here, for the same reason as `DATABASE_URL` above.
# Replace this value; the supplied URL points at a broker on this machine.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# REQUIRED. HMAC key that both signs and verifies the custom (legacy) JWTs in
# `backend/app/core/security.py` - it is used by `create_access_token` and by the
# `legacy_jwt` verification path. The default verification path checks Firebase
# ID tokens instead and does not use this key.
# Overwritten on each `Settings` construction from Secret Manager secret
# `SECRET_KEY`.
# It must still be set here, for the same reason as `DATABASE_URL` above.
# Replace this value with a long random string.
SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string

# REQUIRED. Algorithm used to sign and verify those custom (legacy) JWTs. Not
# overwritten at runtime. `HS256` is a working setting; change it only if your
# deployment signs tokens with a different algorithm.
ALGORITHM=HS256

# REQUIRED. Lifetime in minutes of tokens issued by `create_access_token`, used
# when the caller passes no explicit `expires_delta`. Not overwritten at
# runtime. The supplied value is a working setting and may be tuned.
# SECURITY: token lifetime comes from configuration; it was previously hardcoded.
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=15


# =============================================================================
# SECTION B - Security settings (OPTIONAL, each has a default in config.py)
# =============================================================================
# These have defaults, so the application starts without them. The defaults are
# stated per key. Several must still be set for the application to work
# correctly against real infrastructure, as noted inline.
#
# Key names in this section mirror the `Settings` field declarations, including
# their lower-case spelling.

# Optional, defaults to `[]` (an empty list, which allows no cross-origin
# request). Set this: with the default, the browser SPA is blocked by CORS.
# Must be a JSON array covering local development, staging and production.
# `http://localhost:3000` is the frontend development server. The surrounding
# single quotes keep the JSON double quotes intact when the file is exported
# through a shell; leave them in place.
#
# VALIDATED. Each entry must be an exact origin - `scheme://host` with an
# optional `:port` and nothing else - and the following are rejected at startup:
#   - `*` or any value containing a wildcard
#   - a path, query string or fragment (`https://app.example.com/callback`)
#   - embedded credentials (`https://user:pw@app.example.com`)
#   - a scheme other than http or https, or no scheme at all
#   - plaintext `http://` for anything other than a loopback host
# `http://` is permitted only for `localhost`, `127.0.0.1` and `[::1]`, so the
# frontend development server works while every deployed origin must use https.
# Duplicates are collapsed and surrounding whitespace is trimmed.
#
# Keep this list consistent with the `allowed_origins` Terraform variable in
# `infrastructure/terraform/variables.tf`, which enforces the same grammar.
# SECURITY: explicit CORS allow-list - the origin policy was previously
# undeclared and unresolvable.
# Replace this value; the supplied array covers the development server only.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS='["http://localhost:3000"]'

# Optional, defaults to `""`. Cloud Storage bucket holding user uploads.
# Set this: with the default, upload and download operations fail. Terraform
# names this bucket `<project_id>-user-uploads`. Replace this value.
gcs_bucket_name=your-gcp-project-id-user-uploads

# Optional, defaults to `15`. Lifetime in minutes of each generated V4 signed
# URL, which bounds how long any single object-access grant stays valid.
# Permitted range: 1 to 10080 (seven days), which is the maximum lifetime Cloud
# Storage will sign for. A value outside that range is rejected at startup, not
# at upload time.
# SECURITY: upload URLs are time-limited - they were previously public and
# permanent.
signed_url_expiry_minutes=15

# Optional, defaults to `""`. Email address of the dedicated service account
# that signs upload URLs. Set this: with the default, the runtime's own attached
# identity is used as the signer instead of the dedicated one. Must match the
# Terraform `signer_service_account` variable exactly - that is the identity the
# IAM grant in checklist item 2 below is made against. No key file is involved;
# signing goes through the IAM signBlob API using the runtime's credentials.
# SECURITY: object URLs are signed by a named identity - the signer was
# previously never selected, so signing failed under the deployed credentials.
# Replace this value.
signer_service_account=excel-url-signer@your-gcp-project-id.iam.gserviceaccount.com

# Optional, defaults to `require`. Transport security mode for the PostgreSQL
# connection.
#
# VALIDATED. The only accepted values are `require`, `verify-ca` and
# `verify-full`. PostgreSQL's `disable`, `allow` and `prefer` modes are rejected
# at startup because each of them can complete the connection without
# encryption, which is the condition this setting exists to prevent.
# `verify-ca` and `verify-full` additionally need CA material reachable by the
# client, and `verify-full` requires connecting by the DNS name on the server
# certificate rather than by IP address.
# SECURITY: database traffic is encrypted in transit - the connection was
# previously created with no TLS setting.
db_sslmode=require

# Optional, defaults to `firebase`. Selects the server-side token verification
# path. Values: firebase | legacy_jwt. Consulted per request, so a change takes
# effect on the next request with no restart.
# SECURITY: tokens are verified server-side - client-asserted identity was
# never checked.
auth_token_verifier=firebase

# Optional, defaults to `true`. Break-glass switch only. Setting this to `false`
# bypasses route authentication and leaves every endpoint reachable without a
# valid token; each bypassed request is logged and marked in the response. Keep
# this `true` in every environment, including local development.
# SECURITY: authentication is enforced on every route - previously no route
# required a valid token.
auth_enforcement_enabled=true

# Optional, defaults to `true`. Master switch for both throttling tiers below.
# SECURITY: request volume is bounded - no route was previously rate limited.
rate_limit_enabled=true

# Optional, defaults to `600/minute`. Global per-client request ceiling, as a
# `<count>/<window>` expression. Windows: second | minute | hour | day.
rate_limit_default=600/minute

# Optional, defaults to `120/minute`. Tighter per-client ceiling applied to
# POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE, in the same `<count>/<window>` form. Cell edits
# and autosave both issue writes, so keep this above the write rate a user
# produces while typing; raise it if editing returns HTTP 429.
rate_limit_write=120/minute

# Optional, defaults to `memory://`. Where the throttling windows are counted.
# `memory://` counts inside each worker process, so a client's effective quota is
# multiplied by the number of workers and pods serving the API and resets on
# every restart. Point this at the shared store used by the deployment - for
# example `redis://HOST:6379/1`, the same Redis reachable through `REDIS_URL` but
# on a separate logical database - to make each threshold a true per-client
# limit across the whole service.
# If the configured store is unreachable at startup the application logs a
# warning and falls back to per-process counting; it does not refuse to start.
# SECURITY: throttling windows are shared across workers - per-process counters
# multiplied every client's effective quota.
rate_limit_storage_uri=memory://

# Optional, defaults to `0`. How many trailing entries of `X-Forwarded-For` come
# from proxies you control, counting from the right. `0` ignores the header
# entirely and uses the socket peer address, which is correct when nothing sits
# in front of the application and is the only setting that cannot be spoofed.
# Behind the Google Cloud HTTPS load balancer, which appends the client address
# and its own, set this to the number of hops your ingress path adds. Setting it
# higher than the real hop count lets a caller forge its own throttling identity
# and evade the limits above.
# SECURITY: forwarded client addresses are trusted only for a declared number of
# hops - the header was previously ignored, collapsing every client behind a
# proxy onto one shared quota.
rate_limit_trusted_proxy_hops=0

# Optional, defaults to `false`. When `true`, Content-Security-Policy is sent as
# `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`, which reports violations without
# blocking. When `false`, the policy is enforced. Report-only is only useful
# with `csp_report_uri` set; without a collector it neither blocks nor records.
# SECURITY: a Content-Security-Policy is emitted - no CSP was previously sent on
# any response.
csp_report_only=false

# Optional, defaults to `""` (no collector). Absolute https URL of the endpoint
# that receives Content-Security-Policy violation reports. When set, the policy
# carries `report-uri` and `report-to`, and responses carry a matching
# `Reporting-Endpoints` header. Leave empty and violations go nowhere - which
# makes `csp_report_only=true` silent.
# The application mounts no report endpoint; this must be a collector you run or
# a hosted service.
# SECURITY: policy violations are reported to a collector - they were previously
# neither blocked in report-only mode nor recorded anywhere.
csp_report_uri=

# Optional, defaults to `""`. Firebase / Identity Platform project whose ID
# tokens are accepted. Set this: with the default, the accepted token issuer is
# unconstrained. Usually the same value as `PROJECT_ID`.
# SECURITY: token verification is restricted to a single project - the issuer
# was previously unconstrained.
# Replace this value.
firebase_project_id=your-firebase-project-id

# Optional, defaults to `""`. Exact origin of the API, added to the
# Content-Security-Policy `connect-src` allow-list. Leave it empty ONLY when one
# edge origin serves both the SPA and the API, because the policy's `'self'`
# covers that case already. Otherwise set it: with the default, a browser
# enforcing the policy blocks every API call from the SPA.
#
# It must equal the ORIGIN of the frontend's `REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL`
# (`frontend/src/services/api.ts` builds every request URL from that variable).
# If the frontend build uses `https://api.example.com/v1`, this value is
# `https://api.example.com` - scheme, host and optional port only. A trailing
# slash, path, query string, fragment, userinfo, upper-case host or embedded
# whitespace is rejected at startup. The same grammar governs the Terraform
# `allowed_origins` variable.
# SECURITY: the Content-Security-Policy admits the API origin - the policy
# previously admitted no cross-origin API.
# Replace this value.
api_origin=https://api.your-domain.example.com


# =============================================================================
# SECTION C - Google Cloud client library variables (NOT `Settings` fields)
# =============================================================================
# These are read by the Google Cloud client libraries and the Firebase Admin
# SDK directly, not through `Settings`. They are still required to run the
# backend locally, and they do reach those libraries from this file: the loader
# described at the top of this file puts every key here into the process
# environment, which is where Application Default Credentials looks for them.
# `uvicorn --env-file` and the shell export deliver them the same way.

# How the Firebase Admin SDK resolves the project when no explicit `projectId`
# option and no service-account key are supplied. Present automatically on
# Google infrastructure such as GKE; set it manually for local development.
# Replace this value.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id

# Local development only. Absolute path to a service-account key file, used for
# Application Default Credentials. No key file is required or shipped in the
# deployed container, which uses the runtime's attached identity.
# If set, the path must point to an existing file: a placeholder path makes the
# client libraries fail to start. When authenticating with
# `gcloud auth application-default login`, delete this line.
# Replace this value.
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/your-service-account-key.json


# =============================================================================
# SECTION D - Operator provisioning checklist
# =============================================================================
# These are operator actions, not values to fill in above. Each one blocks
# deployment until it is done.
#
# 1. Create the Secret Manager secrets `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL` and
#    `SECRET_KEY` in project `PROJECT_ID`, each with a `latest` version. This
#    repository creates no secrets. The application reads all three on every
#    `Settings()` construction and fails when they are absent.
#
# 2. Make the runtime identity able to sign V4 signed URLs, before deploying
#    the signed-URL code path. Terraform is the authoritative mechanism for
#    this binding - do not create it by hand.
#
#    `backend/app/services/file_storage.py` signs each URL through the IAM
#    Credentials `signBlob` API, as the service account attached to the runtime
#    - it never reads a private key from the container. So that service account
#    is the signer, and it needs a role containing the
#    `iam.serviceAccounts.signBlob` permission ON ITSELF:
#
#      Resource: the signer service account whose email address you supply in
#                the `signer_service_account` Terraform variable, declared in
#                `infrastructure/terraform/variables.tf`.
#      Role:     `roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator`, bound on that service
#                account's own IAM policy rather than at project level.
#      Member:   the runtime identity the API authenticates as - the service
#                account bound to the backend workload through GKE Workload
#                Identity. The runtime signs as itself, so member and resource
#                are the same account.
#      Effect:   authorizes the `iam.serviceAccounts.signBlob` call that
#                `backend/app/services/file_storage.py` makes through
#                `blob.generate_signed_url`.
#
#    Operator action: set `signer_service_account` and apply
#    `infrastructure/terraform` before the signed-URL code path is deployed.
#    Without the binding, signed-URL generation fails under Application Default
#    Credentials with a missing-private-key error. The equivalent direct command,
#    for reference only:
#
#        gcloud iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding SIGNER_SA_EMAIL \
#          --member="serviceAccount:SIGNER_SA_EMAIL" \
#          --role="roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator"
#
#    Use the same address for `--member` and for the service account being
#    bound, so no other identity is granted signing authority.
#
#    Also enable the IAM Service Account Credentials API in the project
#    (`iamcredentials.googleapis.com`); `signBlob` is served by it.
#
#    Verify with:
#      gcloud iam service-accounts get-iam-policy SIGNER_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL \
#        --flatten='bindings[].members' \
#        --filter='bindings.role:roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator' \
#        --format='value(bindings.members)'
#
#    Locally, `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS` pointing at a service-account key
#    file signs with that key directly and needs neither the binding nor the
#    API. End-user credentials from `gcloud auth application-default login`
#    cannot sign at all, and uploads fail with an explanatory error naming what
#    to use instead.
#
# 3. Keep `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` consistent with the Identity Platform
#    `authorized_domains` list configured in `infrastructure/terraform`. When the
#    two disagree, sign-in and CORS accept different sets of origins.
#
# 4. Provision the managed SSL certificate and confirm it is ACTIVE before
#    cutting the edge forwarding rule over to port 443. Managed certificates
#    require DNS validation and are not immediate.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why any particular default was chosen is recorded in
# `documentation/Security Decision Log.md`.
# =============================================================================
